# mgmoriar.msg - Colin Moriarty (mgmoriar.ssl). One entry per line; no braces in comments.

# 100 to 102: look (stranger, known) and examine.
{100}{}{You see a heavyset man in a waistcoat, counting something.}
{101}{}{You see Colin Moriarty, proprietor.}
{102}{}{He smiles the way a till opens: briefly, and only for money. His eyes add you up and find you a little short.}

# 110 to 113: floats. 114: once the sheriff has died on his floor.
{110}{}{Gob! That glass won't spit on itself.}
{111}{}{Everything in here is for sale. The furniture is for rent.}
{112}{}{A tab is a promise, friends. I collect on promises.}
{113}{}{Drink up. The bomb might go off, and then where's your money's worth?}
{114}{}{Blood on my floor, and who pays for the boards? Nobody. That's who.}

# 150: message window.
{150}{}{You mark a walled city far to the east on your map: Vault City.}

# 200 to 206: greetings.
{200}{}{Welcome to Moriarty's, the finest saloon in Megaton, there being no other. Colin Moriarty. I own the roof, the floor, and most of what goes on between. What'll it be?}
{201}{}{Back again. They always come back. It's the charm, or the lack of anywhere else.}
{202}{}{My favorite kind of customer: one who got what was paid for. Found your vault folk yet?}
{203}{}{Funny thing. My terminal was warm this morning, and I hadn't touched it. I'll not ask who. I'll just watch you a little closer, friend. What'll it be?}
{204}{}{A sheriff shot dead on my own floor. Do you know what that does to trade? ...Doubles it. People are ghouls. What'll it be?}
{205}{}{Well? You've the look of someone with news, or with three hundred dollars. I'd take either.}
{206}{}{What else? Everything's for sale, and the rest is open to negotiation.}

# 210 to 218: hub options (218 for low Intelligence).
{210}{}{I'm looking for a Vault. Or a GECK.}
{211}{}{About that information.}
{212}{}{About Silver.}
{213}{}{Tell me about this town.}
{214}{}{Who's the suit in the corner?}
{215}{}{What became of the man in the suit?}
{216}{}{I need a room.}
{217}{}{Nothing. Goodbye.}
{218}{}{Uhh. Drink?}

# 220 to 235: the quote.
{220}{}{A Vault and a GECK? Someone with the same questions sat at this bar years ago. I can point you after him. A hundred dollars buys the directions now. Come back later and I'll want three hundred.}
{221}{}{The price is one hundred, same as it was. It stays one hundred for exactly as long as you stand there.}
{222}{}{Three hundred now. I told you what walking away costs. I'm a man of my word, when the word is a number.}
{223}{}{Here's a hundred.}
{224}{}{Here's three hundred.}
{225}{}{They say nothing happens that Colin Moriarty doesn't hear. Prove it.}
{226}{}{It was a hundred. A man of his word keeps his first one.}
{227}{}{I can't afford that.}
{228}{}{Too rich for me. Keep it.}
{229}{}{I'll think it over.}
{230}{}{[He laughs, and for once it reaches his eyes.] Flattery. I'd charge for it myself. All right. On the house, since you ask so prettily. Don't tell Gob. He'll want a raise.}
{231}{}{...One hundred, then. But I'll remember that you can count.}
{232}{}{Then it's three hundred, next we speak. Mind the step on your way out.}
{233}{}{[Leave.]}
{234}{}{[He counts it twice, and then once more for pleasure.] There, now. A bargain's a bargain.}
{235}{}{You're short. I can hear it from here. Come back when your pockets are heavier.}

# 240 to 249: the Silver job.
{240}{}{No money. Then you'll work. A girl called Silver poured drinks here till she walked off with three hundred of mine. She camps outside the wall, clear of the caravan fire. Bring me my three hundred and your answer's free.}
{241}{}{I'll find her.}
{242}{}{I'm no debt collector.}
{243}{}{Good. She'll have a story. They always have a story. I want the money, not the story.}
{244}{}{Then earn it somewhere I can't see you, and come back when you have it.}
{246}{}{Then it's cash, like civilized people.}
{247}{}{Then you've a job to be getting on with. Silver. Three hundred. Outside the wall.}
{248}{}{I'll be back.}
{249}{}{[Leave before it gets worse.]}

# 250 to 259: report on Silver.
{250}{}{Well? Where's my three hundred?}
{251}{}{Here. Three hundred.}
{252}{}{[Lie] Her camp was cold. She's gone.}
{253}{}{Silver's dead.}
{254}{}{Still looking.}
{255}{}{Gone. [He looks at you for a long moment.] ...Then it's written off. You tried, and trying is worth nothing. The price of your answer stands.}
{256}{}{Gone, is she. And you standing there with the lie still wet on your lips. The answer's three hundred now. And I still want my money off the girl.}
{257}{}{Dead. Well. The dead pay no debts, and I don't dig. That's the job done for what it was worth, which is nothing. The price of your answer stands.}
{258}{}{[He counts it twice.] Three hundred. There, now. Was that so hard? A bargain's a bargain, and the answer's yours.}
{259}{}{Forget Silver. What's the price in cash?}

# 260 to 263: the information.
{260}{}{Years back a man came through in a blue jumpsuit, a yellow number on his back, asking for the same Vault and the same box. He went east, to a walled city run by vault folk. Clean as a scalpel, I hear, and about as warm.}
{261}{}{Vault City, they call it, with all the imagination you'd expect. He never came back to say what he found. That tells you something, or it tells you nothing. Mind how you go.}
{262}{}{East. A walled city. What's it called?}
{263}{}{I'll find it.}

# 270 to 274: the town.
{270}{}{My father's money raised that wall, and mine keeps the taps running. Simms wears a hat and calls it a law. The Stahls water their beer and call it a restaurant. And everybody drinks here. You tell me whose town it is.}
{271}{}{And the bomb?}
{272}{}{Hasn't gone off in my lifetime. If it does, I'll not be about to refund anyone. The church brings pilgrims, and pilgrims drink. Leave it be.}
{273}{}{Dead as a doornail, they tell me, thanks to some meddler. The faithful are drinking more for it. You'll hear no complaint out of me.}
{274}{}{Something else.}

# 280 to 283: Mister Burke.
{280}{}{Mister Burke. Pays for his room in advance, in pre-War paper crisp as the day it was printed. That much is free. Anything more about him costs more than you have.}
{281}{}{Checked out. Left a tip, too: the first in the history of the establishment. I'm having it framed.}
{282}{}{The one who put a hole in our sheriff? Out the gate before the body cooled. I'm billing the town for the floorboards.}
{283}{}{Dead, and his room paid up to the end of the month. He tipped better alive, I'll say that for him.}

# 290, 291: a room.
{290}{}{Rooms are Nova's department. A hundred and twenty, up front, and she sees none of it worth mentioning. Go and be charming at her.}
{291}{}{The room's vacant. So is the position, if you know anyone.}

# 295, 296: low Intelligence.
{295}{}{[He looks at you, then past you, for whoever you belong to.] Gob! Customer. Of a sort.}
{296}{}{Drink!}
